#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa6b152-86d3-5f96-8a94-57f86a17a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Over a Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "656ea4bb-04c7-53fa-ae19-e02f1bb423db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355856} {:answer/id #uuid "89607af9-a96f-5710-950d-43d5b27cd967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355857} {:answer/id #uuid "544f945e-1948-54fa-9a69-6265f16fee7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355858} {:answer/id #uuid "d26f0f79-3b55-5d36-a0f4-e27e53b47ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355859} {:answer/id #uuid "57146f5c-731b-5aeb-918e-8bfe4c3afbde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355860} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd88d8b-1154-5924-84b2-a8255f4e1264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cd88d8b-1154-5924-84b2-a8255f4e1264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355890} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b0d1580-cb70-50e0-aeb7-fd5dcae8bb21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75f60136-1ae9-5f0b-8a88-b99b9aa1fce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161481" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$\n\nInterpret the printed inequalities literally. If they define a degenerate set, use the zero-area surface-integral convention. What is the flux?\n\nHint: First check whether $-x\\le y\\le0$ can have a nonempty interval when $x\\le0$. Treat the printed inequalities literally." :question/worked-solution "The original inequalities are retained literally. Since $-\\pi\\le x\\le0$, we have $-x\\ge0$. The additional inequalities $-x\\le y\\le0$ are possible only when $-x=0$, so $x=0$ and $y=0$. Therefore the projection is $D=\\{(0,0)\\}$ and its two-dimensional area is zero. The graph $z=\\cos(x+y)$ over $D$ consists only of $(0,0,1)$; it is a degenerate surface, not a regular two-dimensional patch.\n\nUnder the zero-area surface-integral convention, both its surface area and its flux are zero:\n\n$$\\operatorname{area}(S)=0,\\qquad\\iint_S\\langle1,0,y\\rangle\\cdot d\\mathbf S=0.$$\n\nOne may extend the upward graph vector area element from the surrounding smooth graph: $(-g_x,-g_y,1)=(\\sin(x+y),\\sin(x+y),1)$. Its dot product with $F=\\langle1,0,y\\rangle$ is $\\sin(x+y)+y$. Integrating this bounded continuous function over the literal zero-area set $D$ yields zero.\n\nThe source accepted choice b, $\\pi-\\pi^3/6$, and its revealed solution instead evaluated the formal signed repeated integral\n\n$$\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\int_{-x}^{0}(\\sin(x+y)+y)\\,dy\\,dx=\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\left(1-\\cos x-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)\\,dx=\\pi-\\frac{\\pi^3}{6}.$$\n\nFor $x<0$, these inner limits are reversed. A formal signed integral over reversed limits does not integrate over the region described by the original inequalities. No corrected bounds are inferred. The study answer is $0$ for the original literal inequalities; the source's choice, grading and calculation remain preserved as evidence."}]}
